    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks. one more thing, regarding the screen reader part—sometimes when we run tests, we find these weird little bugs where the focus jumps around or labels just don't show up when they should. any tips on how to hunt those down or prevent them from popping up during the build?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "To resolve these issues with screen reader focus jumping or missing labels, consider the following tips:\n\n- Use CSS to provide a tabindex attribute on interactive elements and the wrapper container where they're nested, so optional focus traversal is enabled.\n- Set a logical focus interaction for buttons, forms, and other interactive elements, using ARIA attributes like aria-setactivedescendant and aria-endsitedescendant.\n\nTo prevent or minimize such bugs during the build, try these steps:\n\n- Instrument your build process with logging statements or testing tools to identify where these issues occur.\n- Create targeted Accessibility Audits to focus on modes where these issues tend to appear (e.g., on small screens, when interacting with particular UI elements).\n- Perform thorough unit and UI testing in code to prove early on that these edge cases aren't encountered.\n\nIt's worth mentioning that 'applying' these accessibility suggestions can often change how the interactions tested by users (and screen readers and automated test tools) work, but ultimately improve the user experience for those with disabilities to build both functional and usable apps.",
